Atque his omnia affectuum remedia, sive id omne, quod Mens, in se sola considerata, adversus affectus potest, comprehendi ; ex quibus apparet, Mentis in affectus potentiam consistere, 
I° in ipsa affectuum cognitione (vide Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; 
II° in eo, quod affectus a cogitatione causæ externæ, quam confuse imaginamur, separat (vide Prop. 2 cum eodem Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; 
III° in tempore, quo affectiones, quæ ad res, quas intelligimus referuntur, illas superant, quæ ad res referuntur, quas confuse seu mutilate concipimus (vide Prop. 7 hujus) ; 
IV° in multitudine causarum, a quibus affectiones, quæ ad rerum communes proprietates vel ad Deum referuntur, foventur (vide Prop. 9 et 11 hujus) ; 
V° denique in ordine, quo Mens suos affectus ordinare et invicem concatenare potest (vide Schol. Prop. 10 et insuper Prop. 12, 13 et 14 hujus).

And in these are comprehended all the remedies for the affects, or all that the Mind, considered in itself alone, can do against the affects; from which it is apparent that the power of the Mind over the affects consists,
I° in the knowledge of the affects themselves (see the Schol. to Prop. 4 of this Part);
II° in its separating an affect from the thought of an external cause which we imagine confusedly (see Prop. 2 together with the same Schol. to Prop. 4 of this Part);
III° in the time by which affections that are referred to things which we understand surpass those that are referred to things which we conceive confusedly or mutilatedly (see Prop. 7 of this Part);
IV° in the multitude of causes by which affections that are referred to the common properties of things or to God are nourished (see Props. 9 and 11 of this Part);
V° finally, in the order by which the Mind can order and concatenate its affects with one another (see the Schol. to Prop. 10 and moreover Props. 12, 13, and 14 of this Part).

