For since those born blind, who have never [seen] the light of the sun

though they have discerned them, nevertheless recognize bodies by touch

as joined from their earliest age with no color,

you may know that bodies too can come to the knowledge of our mind

without being smeared round about with any coloring.

Finally, whatever things we ourselves touch in blind

darkness, we do not perceive as dyed with any color.

Since I have shown that this happens, I shall now teach that they are [colorless].

For every color is wholly changed into every [other color];

and the first-beginnings ought by no means to do this;

for something unchangeable must remain over,

lest all things be utterly reduced to nothing;

for whatever has changed and passes beyond its own limits,

at once this is the death of that which it was before.

Therefore take care not to endow the seeds of things with color,

lest all things utterly return for you to nothing.

Thus a threefold nature of the spirit has now been discovered;

yet all these are not sufficient to create sensation.

since the mind admits that it can create none of these things,

the sentient motions which, in short, it turns over in the mind.

Therefore a certain fourth nature too must necessarily

be assigned to these; it is altogether without a name;

than which nothing exists more mobile or more subtle,

nor made more of small and light elements;

it first distributes sentient motions through the limbs.

For it is first set in motion, being composed of tiny shapes;

then heat receives motion, and the blind power of wind,

then air, then all things are set in motion:

the blood is shaken, then all the viscera perceive the sensation;

last of all it is given to the bones and marrow,

whether it be pleasure or a contrary burning pain.

But it is not possible to say why they themselves act, or why they toil;

for, when they are without a body, they do not

flit about troubled by diseases and cold and hunger;

for the body suffers more from these defects,

and the mind performs many evils through contagion with it.

Yet grant that, when they enter, it is however useful for them to make a body;

but no way by which they can do so is apparent.

Therefore souls do not make bodies and limbs for themselves.

Nor yet is it possible that they are insinuated into bodies

already complete; for they will not be able to be subtly

connected, nor will contacts occur by mutual accord.

Finally, why does the fierce violence of grim lions

follow their breed, cunning the fox, and flight the deer?

It is given from their fathers, and hereditary fear stirs their limbs,

and now why besides do all other things of this kind in their limbs

are generated from the beginning of life, and in disposition,

if not, because by a fixed seed and seminal power of its own

the force of mind likewise grows together with the body?

But if it were immortal and accustomed to change

bodies, living creatures would be of intermingled natures;

a dog of Hyrcanian breed would often flee

the attack of a horned stag, and would tremble through the breezes;

a hawk, fleeing through the air, at the approach of a dove;

men would be mad, the wild generations of beasts would be wise.

For this is advanced by false reasoning, which they say:

that an immortal soul is altered when the body is changed;

for whatever is changed is dissolved, and therefore perishes;

for its parts are shifted and migrate from their order;

wherefore they must also be able to be dissolved throughout the limbs,

and finally all perish together with the body.

They tell of other prodigies and portents of this kind,

lest perhaps deserted places too should be thought to be occupied by gods alone.

Therefore they spread miracles in their tales,

or are led by some other reason, since the whole

human race is excessively greedy for ears.

For the rest, it is not wonderful by what means,

through places where the eyes cannot behold things openly,

things come through those places by voices and strike the ears,

since we often see conversation even through closed doors;

for voice can pass unharmed through the winding openings of things,

while images refuse to do so;

for they are torn apart unless they pass through straight openings,

such as those of glass, through which every image flies.

For one thing moves and excites one thing, another another;

from a human being, the force of a human being alone draws forth human seed.

As soon as it has been cast out from its own seats, it goes forth

from the whole body through the limbs and members,

gathering in the appointed places of the sinews and immediately exciting

the very regions of the generative part of the body.

The irritated places swell with seed, and there arises the desire

to cast it forth whither fierce lust strains;

and it seeks the body whence the mind is wounded by love.

For all generally fall toward the wound, and the blood leaps

toward that quarter whence we are struck by the blow;

and, if he is near at hand, the red fluid occupies the foe.

multaque  subita et paupertas horrida suasit;



namque suos consanguineos aliena rogorum



insuper extructa ingenti clamore locabant



subdebantque faces, multo cum sanguine saepe



rixantes, potius quam corpora desererentur,



inque aliis alium populum sepelire suorum



certantes; lacrimis lassi luctuque redibant;



inde bonam partem in lectum maerore dabantur;



nec poterat quisquam reperiri, quem neque morbus



nec mors nec luctus temptaret tempore tali.

And many sudden acts did dreadful poverty prompt;

for upon alien funeral-pyres, built up above their own kinsmen,

they placed them with a great outcry

and put torches beneath them, often fighting

with much bloodshed rather than leaving the bodies behind;

and, striving to bury one man upon another's pyres belonging to their own people,

they returned wearied by tears and mourning;

then in large part they gave themselves over to bed in grief.

Nor could anyone be found whom neither disease

nor death nor mourning assailed at such a time.
